227 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Based on our projections derived from the company's valuation model, we forecast that Coca-Cola's free cash flow to the firm \(FCFF\) will demonstrate robust growth, rising from approximately $14.0 billion in 2025 to over $20.0 billion by the end of the forecast period in 2029. This powerful and predictable cash generation is the lifeblood of the company's shareholder return policy, enabling it to consistently fund its generous dividend, engage in regular share repurchase programs, and retain sufficient capital for strategic, bolt-on acquisitions to fuel future growth.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
228 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (From a profitability perspective, Coca-Cola's operating model is designed for high margins. We project that its earnings before interest and taxes \(EBIT\) will grow steadily from $15.3 billion in 2025 to a forecasted $22.6 billion in 2029. This growth reflects the company's significant pricing power, the inherent margin advantage of its concentrate business model, and ongoing efforts to drive operational efficiencies throughout its system. While the company is not immune to margin pressures from volatile commodity prices \(such as sugar and aluminum\) and the need for significant marketing investment to maintain brand health, its immense scale and purchasing power provide a substantial buffer against these headwinds.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
229 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The company's balance sheet is characteristic of a mature blue-chip firm. It maintains a significant level of debt, reported at $44.5 billion, which is used strategically to finance its global operations and value-accretive acquisitions. This debt load is managed prudently and is comfortably supported by the company's massive and stable cash flows, as evidenced by its strong investment-grade credit ratings. The company also holds a substantial cash and investments balance, providing it with ample liquidity and financial flexibility.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
