/E (One of the most significant risks facing Coca-Cola is the sustained increase in key input costs, particularly sugar, aluminum for cans, and transportation expenses. Commodity price inflation has been exacerbated by global supply chain disruptions and geopolitical tensions, resulting in higher raw material prices. Although Coca-Cola has historically demonstrated strong pricing power, the ability to pass on cost increases to consumers is limited by competitive pressures and price sensitivity in key markets. Failure to fully offset these rising costs could compress EBITDA margins and reduce overall profitability, posing a material risk to financial performance.)
